<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Shapsugs</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Shapsugs are a Circassian people of the north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caucasus">Caucasus</a> who lived along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a> coast and the northern slopes of the mountains between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anapa">Anapa</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sochi">Sochi</a> area. Among the largest western Circassian communities, they broke the power of their nobles in the late eighteenth century and were governed by assemblies. Most were expelled to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> in 1864; a small community remains near Sochi.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/shapsugs.jpg" alt="Shapsugs" width="320" height="121" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Yahya Kuadzhe · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Shapsug_Banner.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The Shapsugs are one of the twelve major Circassian tribes and were historically among the largest groups of the Black Sea Adyghe. The Little Shapsug lived between the Dzhubga and Shakhe rivers, while the Greater Shapsug held high mountain country on the northern slopes of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caucasus">Caucasus</a>. They controlled the ports of Dzhubga and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tuapse">Tuapse</a> and the gorges behind them. Before the Russo-Caucasian War, sources put their number between 150,000 and 300,000.</p><p>The tribe consisted of five aristocratic families and 81 free clans, and it is classed as one of the three democratic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/circassians">Adyghe</a> tribes. The Shapsugs supported other Adyghe tribes in their struggle against the Crimean Khanate. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caucasian-war">Caucasian War</a> they were among the most stubborn opponents of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Imperial Russia</a> and joined the alliance of Shamil, which lasted until 1859. In late 1860 representatives of three Adyghe tribes, the Shapsug, Ubykh and Natukhai, met in a council that named <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sochi">Sochi</a> the last capital of the Circassian resistance.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>Traditional Shapsug culture had much in common with that of the other Circassian tribes. The Shapsugs practised agriculture, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/horse-breeding">horse breeding</a>, horticulture, viticulture and beekeeping. In pre-Islamic and pre-Christian times they worshipped Circassian gods, among them Shible, god of thunder and lightning, Sozeresh, god of fertility, Tlepsh, god of blacksmithing, and Keshkogwasha, god of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a>. In droughts they performed the Hantse Guashe ceremony, carrying a dressed doll through the village and drowning it in the river. Since the early 19th century they have been mainly Sunni Muslims of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanafi-school">Hanafi school</a>. Their speech is a sub-dialect of the West Adyghe dialect of Adyghe, and before the exile there were two major varieties of it.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The Shapsugs were among the last to lay down their weapons in 1864, and some fought on into the 1880s. After the war the overwhelming majority were forcibly evicted to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> and elsewhere in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-east">Middle East</a>, and only a small part of the tribe stayed on its native land. On 6 September 1924 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolsheviks">Bolsheviks</a> created a Shapsug national district of about 3,400 people, which was renamed Lazarevsky district in 1945. A resolution of 12 June 1992 re-established it, after the tribe's first congress in 1990.</p><p>Today the largest Shapsug communities are in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>, with others in Israel, Jordan, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lebanon">Lebanon</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a>. The first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/circassians">Circassians</a> to settle in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amman">Amman</a> were Shapsugs. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> of 2002 counted 3,231 Shapsugs.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Shapsugs” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Among the <b>Shapsugs</b>, Natukhaj and Abadzekhs of the west, the power of princes and nobles was weaker and largely broken in the late eighteenth century, and free communities governed themselves.</blockquote><a href="/empire/circassia">Circassia · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
